            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A CVS result at 12 weeks shows trisomy 21, but the obstetrician recommends confirmatory amniocentesis. What is the most likely reason?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Maternal cell contamination of the villus sample</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Maternal cell contamination is primarily a concern in amniocentesis, where the first 1–2 mL of aspirated fluid are discarded to prevent it. It is not the typical reason for ordering confirmatory testing after a CVS result.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Confined placental mosaicism producing a false-positive result</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Confined placental mosaicism occurs when the chorionic villi carry a chromosomal change that is absent in the fetus itself. This CVS-specific phenomenon can produce false-positive results, necessitating amniocentesis to confirm the fetal karyotype.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The CVS was performed too early, before 10 weeks of gestation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CVS before 10 weeks carries a risk of limb reduction defects, but this procedure was performed at 12 weeks — well within the optimal 10–13-week window. Timing alone does not prompt confirmatory amniocentesis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The direct preparation method is unreliable for detecting trisomies</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The direct preparation gives rapid results (24–48 hours) and is suitable for trisomy detection. The need for confirmatory amniocentesis arises from the possibility of confined placental mosaicism, not from concerns about the laboratory method.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is confined placental mosaicism. Because CVS samples placental tissue rather than fetal cells directly, the chromosomal makeup of the villi may occasionally differ from that of the fetus — a phenomenon called confined placental mosaicism."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Confined placental mosaicism is a key limitation unique to CVS — the placental villi may carry chromosomal changes not present in the fetus, requiring amniocentesis for definitive confirmation."
                }
              ],
